My grandmother would always say that this is Sarah's brother's act. The sharing food is important, but I'd say the most important part is being around the table. But food was the lure to get everyone up. Yeah, exactly. We kept us there. Yeah, exactly. Food brought us together, but it was the company and the conversation.
我祖母常说，这是萨拉哥哥的行为。分享食物很重要，但我认为最重要的事情是围坐在一起。不过食物是把大家吸引过来的诱惑。对，没错。它把我们留在了那里。对，没错。食物把我们凝聚在一起，但维系我们的却是陪伴和交谈。

4
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Today on Hidden Brain, the profound role that food plays in our lives. Food is not just nutrition that goes in your mouth or even pleasant sensations that go with it. It connects to your whole life. We look at the culture and psychology that determine what we eat, what we spit out, and when we come back for more. Paul Rosen has been studying the psychology and culture of food for more than 40 years.
今天在《隐藏的大脑》中，我们将探讨食物在我们生活中扮演的深远角色。食物不仅仅是送进你嘴里的营养，甚至也不仅仅是与之相伴的美妙感官体验。它与你的整个人生息息相关。我们将探讨决定我们吃什么、吐什么以及什么时候想再来一份的文化与心理学。保罗·罗赞研究食物的心理学和文化已经超过40年了。

5
00:01:45.000 --> 00:02:38.000
He works at the University of Pennsylvania. Early in his career, Paul found himself pondering a question that few of us might think to ask, why do so many people across the world enjoy the hot, stinging pain of chili peppers? This question took Paul to a small village in Mexico, where chili peppers were as common as salt and pepper in the United States. They don't think their food tastes good without it, and the little kids don't like it. So something happens somewhere between two and five years of age. At the meals where everybody adult is eating the hot pepper and the older children are, and they're enjoying it and a little kid is thinking it's terrible and after a while some magic occurs and the little kids like it. So I thought well we had there a number of possible accounts.
他在宾夕法尼亚大学工作。在他的职业生涯早期，保罗发现自己陷入了一个我们当中很少有人会去想的问题：为什么世界上会有那么多人喜欢辣椒那种火辣、刺痛的痛感？这个问题把保罗带到了墨西哥的一个小村庄，在那里，辣椒就像美国的盐和胡椒一样普遍。他们觉得没有辣椒的食物不好吃，而小孩子则不喜欢它。所以，在两岁到五岁之间的某个时候，有些事情发生了变化。在所有成年人和大龄儿童都在吃辣椒、享受辣椒的饭桌上，小孩子却觉得这太糟糕了，而过了一段时间，某种神奇的事情发生了，小孩子们也开始喜欢它了。所以我想，我们对此有几种可能的解释。

6
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:57.000
Some accounts involve something fundamentally biological. And presumably the biological explanation would be if you start eating it long enough, you're eventually just going to like it for biological reasons. Yes, that's right. The brain compensates for all sorts of things. We adapt to things. This is a case of more than adaptation, where you're turning something that's negative into something positive.
有些解释涉及某种根本上的生物学机制。据推测，生物学上的解释会是：如果你开始吃的时间足够长，出于生物学的原因，你最终自然就会喜欢上它。是的，没。错。大脑会对各种事情进行补偿。我们会适应各种事情。这不仅是适应的问题，你是在把某种消极的东西变成积极的东西。

7
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:23.000
I mean, to me, that's an amazing thing that we can start with something that's innately negative and make it really positive. So I said, let's take a look at the animals in the village because the dogs and the pigs eat Mexican food. They're eating tacos and hot sauce and beans and all of that's love. So I went around the village and I first asked people, do you have any dogs or pigs that like hot pepper? And they said, what are you crazy? I said, well, yeah, I mean they eat it.
我的意思是，对我来说，这是一件了不起的事：我们可以从某种天生消极的东西开始，把它变成非常积极的东西。所以我说，让我们来看看村子里的动物，因为这里的狗和猪也吃墨西哥食物。它们吃塔可、辣酱、豆子，所有这些都是爱。所以我走遍了全村，首先问人们：“你们有没有喜欢吃辣椒的狗或猪？”他们说：“你疯了吗？”我说：“好吧，我是说，它们确实吃啊。”

