where the kids are eating with their parents and their older siblings, and they keep eating it because the social pressure to eat it. Now, the animals keep eating it because the social pressure to eat it. Now, the animals keep eating it too because they're hungry, so it can't be just that they keep eating it. So, it's really, to me, a miracle. And what I realized is that it's a miracle that takes place in humans all over the world,
孩子们和父母以及较大的兄弟姐妹一起吃饭，他们不断地吃，是因为吃东西的社会压力。现在，动物们也在继续吃，因为吃东西的社会压力。现在，动物们饿了也会继续吃，所以不能仅仅归咎于它们一直在吃。所以，对我来说，这真是一个奇迹。我意识到，这是一个发生在世界各地人类身上的奇迹，

12
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:35.000
not just about hot pepper. It's also about liking coffee, which is bitter, and people don't like it originally, and they like it. And it goes outside the food domain. People like to go on roller coasters. Now, a roller coaster is a very negative experience the first time you have it, right? You think you're crashing to death, and your heart is pounding, and yet people pay to do this. Can you imagine a dog paying to go on a roller coaster? I mean, you know, it just won't happen. So we found that there's a whole range of things in which humans enjoy what is originally negative and they come to enjoy it as positive.
不仅仅是关于辣椒。它也关乎喜欢咖啡，咖啡是苦的，人们一开始并不喜欢它，但后来却喜欢上了它。而且它超出了食物的范畴。人们喜欢坐过山车。现在，第一次坐过山车是非常糟糕的体验，对吧？你觉得自己要坠毁身亡了，心跳加速，然而人们却花钱去体验这个。你能想象一只狗花钱去坐过山车吗？我是说，你知道的，这根本不可能发生。所以我们发现，人类有一系列的事情，他们会享受那些最初是负面的东西，并最终将它们视为积极的享受。

13
00:05:35.000 --> 00:06:12.000
This includes the fatigue of running, it includes said movies, it includes being afraid in horror movies. This seems to be only humans. So our thought, and it's only a thought, is that what humans are enjoying is the very fact that their body thinks that something is bad, but they know it's okay. We call it benign masochism. The human have this special ability to appreciate the fact that they know that something that their body is saying is bad is actually good. And we have evidence for that. So for example, for chili pepper, the favorite degree of hotness is just below the level of unbearable pain.
这包括跑步的疲劳，包括悲伤的电影，包括在恐怖电影中感到害怕。这似乎是人类独有的。所以我们的想法——仅仅是一个想法——是人类真正享受的事实是：他们的身体认为某事是不好的，但他们知道其实没事。我们称之为良性虐恋。人类具有这种特殊的能力，能够欣赏这样一件事：他们知道身体发出的“情况不妙”的信号其实是安全的。我们有证据支持这一点。比如，对于辣椒来说，最受欢迎的辣度刚好在无法忍受的痛感之下。

