You're no longer liking it just because of the pure pressure or the social interactions you're liking it because it gives you a sense that you're coming close to the edge of something thrilling. Is that the argument? Yes, there's something thrilling that is not threatening. Right. So for example, if something is threatening, you don't get to like it. Like people don't get to like serious pain. Right. It has to have something of the sense of it's not really threatening. And it even becomes funny. So in the case of disgust, people don't like disgusting things, right?
你不再仅仅因为同辈压力或社会交往而喜欢它，你喜欢它是因为它给你带来了一种感觉：你正接近某种惊险刺激的边缘。是这个论点吗？是的，有一种令人兴奋且不具威胁性的刺激。没错。例如，如果某事具有威胁性，你就不会喜欢它。就像人们不会喜欢严重的疼痛一样。没错。它必须带有一种“并非真的具有威胁性”的感觉。它甚至变得很有趣。所以在厌恶感方面，人们不喜欢令人厌恶的东西，对吧？

